ocal races like the 2nd District but shows no sign of moving the statewide presidential vote"], "rationale": "Arkansas's deep Republican lean and the absence of any polling, ratings, or market signals pointing to movement keep the race firmly in Republican territory; the open seat gives neither side an incumbency edge, and Democratic strength in the Little Rock-based House district does not extend to statewide presidential contests.", "watch": "A nationally competitive Democratic nominee or a large shift in the national political environment could narrow the margin, though Arkansas would likely remain Republican.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No expert ratings published yet; forecast rests on fundamentals and polls.", "New or vacant seat with no expert ratings; fundamentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "Arkansas remains one of the most Republican-leaning states in presidential politics, with a partisan lean of roughly fifteen points toward the GOP. With the incumbent president term-limited, both parties will nominate new candidates, and no incumbency advantage applies to either side. No public polls of the Arkansas presidential race, expert ratings, or market prices are available, so the state's underlying partisanship drives the outlook.\n\nThe Democratic path would require a major national shift, since even the most Democratic-leaning corner of the state, the Little Rock-based 2nd District, is only modestly competitive in downballot races. Local headlines show Democratic energy in congressional contests, including an endorsement from Kamala Harris, but nothing indicating a statewide presidential change. Arkansas is expected to back the Republican nominee by a wide margin, with uncertainty somewhat elevated because polling is absent.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0496, "margin": -15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0891, "margin": -15.0}, {"date": "2026-09